* text=auto

# Golden files for byte-identity regression tests. Treat as binary so
# autocrlf does not rewrite line endings between commit and checkout.
tests/golden/** binary

# The frozen input of the published validation study (F-98). Same reason
# as above, and load-bearing for a different one: these bytes are pinned
# by docs/data/study_input/SHA256SUMS, so a checkout that rewrote their
# line endings would fail the integrity check and look like tampering.
# Three of the four are pure-LF and one is pure-CRLF.
docs/data/study_input/*.csv binary

# The evidence behind docs/llm-evaluation.md § "Local models on this corpus"
# (F-159). Pinned by docs/data/wave12_local_runs/SHA256SUMS *and* by the
# sha256 block inside each run's own manifest.json, so these bytes have to
# survive checkout unaltered or both checks fail at once. The EL_FULL.csv
# files are the sharp case: they use CRLF row terminators with bare LFs
# inside quoted abstracts, which is exactly the mixed content `* text=auto`
# normalises — without this rule a fresh clone rewrites them and every
# digest in the directory breaks.
docs/data/wave12_local_runs/*.csv binary
docs/data/wave12_local_runs/*.jsonl binary
docs/data/wave12_local_runs/*.json binary

# The evidence behind register row F-197 and the § 8.3 retraction in
# docs/internal/diagnostic/07_criteria_parsing.md (wave 14c) — same reason
# and same shape as the wave12_local_runs rules above: the EL_FULL.csv
# files mix CRLF row terminators with bare LFs inside quoted abstracts,
# which is exactly what `* text=auto` normalises, and every byte here is
# pinned by docs/data/wave14c_batch_runs/SHA256SUMS and asserted by
# tests/test_wave14c_batch_freeze.py.
docs/data/wave14c_batch_runs/*.csv binary
docs/data/wave14c_batch_runs/*.jsonl binary
docs/data/wave14c_batch_runs/*.json binary

# The evidence behind register rows F-201 / F-197's invariance measurement
# (wave 14d) — same reason and shape as the two evidence directories above.
# No *.jsonl rule: these four runs were made with the cache OFF, so no
# bundle contains a cache member and none is committed (the absence is
# itself evidence — see the directory's meta file).
docs/data/wave14d_invariance_runs/*.csv binary
docs/data/wave14d_invariance_runs/*.json binary

# The evidence behind wave 15e's acceptance experiment (the F-195
# settlement, and the annotations on F-201/F-197) — same reason and shape
# as the directories above. Cache OFF again, so again no *.jsonl rule.
docs/data/wave15e_acceptance_runs/*.csv binary
docs/data/wave15e_acceptance_runs/*.json binary

# The evidence behind wave 16b's eight-arm criteria-diversity experiment —
# same reason and shape as the directories above. The `**` reaches
# aborted_attempt1/, whose artefacts are preserved rather than deleted and
# are pinned by the same SHA256SUMS. Cache OFF in every arm, so again no
# *.jsonl rule.
#
# The *.md rule is wave 16d's correction. Wave 16b left the aborted_attempt1
# README to `* text=auto` on the reasoning that it is "prose about the bytes
# rather than evidence" — but it was also listed in SHA256SUMS, and a
# recorded digest is a promise about bytes. On Windows the very next
# checkout rewrote it to CRLF and its digest stopped matching, a failure
# invisible on Linux CI and masked on Windows CI by the bijection error that
# fired first. **The rule this encodes: if a file's digest is recorded, its
# bytes must be attribute-protected — no exceptions for prose.**
docs/data/wave16_live_runs/**/*.csv binary
docs/data/wave16_live_runs/**/*.json binary
docs/data/wave16_live_runs/**/*.txt binary
docs/data/wave16_live_runs/**/*.log binary
docs/data/wave16_live_runs/**/*.md binary

# Every SHA256SUMS is the pinning mechanism for a frozen directory, and it
# had the same hole for a subtler reason: it has no extension, so every
# glob-shaped rule above missed it and it resolved to `text: auto` in all
# six frozen directories at once. The freeze readers parse it
# line-ending-robustly, so nothing was broken today — but a file that
# certifies bytes should not itself be rewritable on checkout. One rule for
# every frozen directory, present and future.
docs/data/**/SHA256SUMS binary

# The *outputs* of the reproduction the rule above protects the inputs of
# (F-99, cause 2 of the wave-10..12 red CI). These three are the published
# artefacts that tests/test_study_input_freeze.py, class
# TestTheFrozenInputReproducesThePublishedResults, compares byte-for-byte
# against a fresh run of tools/eval_ingest.py.
# The blobs are LF; the ingestor writes LF on every platform
# (newline="" plus lineterminator="\n"); the only thing that produced CRLF
# was this file's own `* text=auto` acting on checkout. So a fresh clone on
# Windows failed three tests while every working tree that predated the rule
# passed — which is why it survived two days and three wave wrap-ups.
#
# The glob is `*.csv` and must stay `*.csv`. `docs/data/*` would also catch
# eval_summary_v1.txt, which is LF in the index, CRLF in this working tree,
# and byte-identical on every platform *only* because its writer
# (eval_ingest.py::write_summary_text) uses Path.write_text with no
# newline= and is therefore platform-native — it matches whatever the
# checkout produced. Pinning it to LF while its writer emits CRLF on Windows
# would invert the failure rather than remove it. Measured both ways.
#
# Note that `*` does not cross `/` in a gitattributes pattern, so this rule
# neither subsumes nor duplicates the two directory rules above, and
# docs/data/grids/partition_manifest.csv stays `text: auto` deliberately:
# it is an input to the reproduction, never one of its compared outputs.
docs/data/*.csv binary

# The demonstration corpus, pinned CRLF rather than binary — the opposite
# direction from every rule above, and the direction matters (F-99, cause 3
# of the same red CI; it was masked by the httpx failure until wave 13a
# fixed that and unmasked it on all twelve non-Windows cells).
#
# runB_llama32_manifest.json and runC_qwen25_manifest.json record
# `data/original.csv` as b36c3cbb…, which is the sha256 of this file **as
# CRLF**. Those manifests are evidence of what the wave-12 runs actually
# consumed: the runs were made on Windows, against the checkout-converted
# copy. They are not to be recomputed to suit a test.
# tests/test_wave12_measurement_freeze.py, class
# TestTheOmittedBundleMembersAreAccountedFor, hashes this file live and
# compares. Under `* text=auto` the index's LF reaches a Linux worktree
# unconverted, giving 311442a0… and a failure on every non-Windows cell.
#
# `eol=crlf` makes the checkout produce on every platform the bytes the
# manifests were computed from. **F-99's own suggested fix, `samples/**
# binary`, is the exact inverse and must not be implemented as written:**
# `binary` pins the *index* bytes, which are LF, so it would make Windows
# compute 311442a0… and break the four cells that currently pass. Measured
# both ways. F-99's fix cell records the correction.
#
# Scoped to the one file whose bytes an external manifest pins. The other
# three files under samples/ are hashed nowhere, and widening the rule
# would change what a Linux reader sees for no stated reason. If another
# sample ever becomes hash-pinned it needs its own line here — and the
# guard in tests/test_wave12_measurement_freeze.py derives the pinned set
# from the manifests rather than repeating it, so it will say so.
#
# MIGRATION, for an existing clone rather than a fresh one. An attribute
# change only takes effect when git rewrites the file, and this commit
# changes no blob — so a Linux or macOS working tree that predates it keeps
# its LF copy, `git status` stays clean (the dirty check normalises before
# comparing), and the two freeze tests fail while blaming the manifests.
# One `git checkout -- samples/` fixes it; it rewrites the working-tree
# file and changes no blob. CI is unaffected: actions/checkout is always a
# fresh checkout.
#
# KEEP A TRAILING NEWLINE AFTER THE LAST RULE. Without one, `>>` merges the
# next rule into this line and git reads the result as
# `eol=crlfsamples/*.txt binary` — which sets text=unset, i.e. exactly the
# `binary` inversion this comment exists to warn against. Measured.
samples/20260122_1654_aggregate.csv text eol=crlf

# The RSA corpus (wave 17a), pinned the same way and for the same reason as
# the line above — but it needed a byte fix FIRST, and the order matters.
#
# As harvested it carried 464 CRLF row terminators, 12 CRLF *inside* quoted
# abstracts, and 36 BARE LFs also inside quoted abstracts. That mix is
# exactly what `* text=auto` normalises: the index would hold every newline
# as LF, and a Windows checkout would convert all of them back to CRLF —
# including the 36 that were never CRLF to begin with. The file came back
# 36 bytes longer (857,600 -> 857,636) with a different sha256. Measured:
# eb01aca5… on disk, 2c1e2e9b… in the index, e5bf3ab1… after checkout.
#
# `eol=crlf` alone does NOT fix that, and this is the trap worth naming:
# the rule above works only because its file has ZERO bare LFs, so LF->CRLF
# on checkout reproduces the original exactly. Copying the rule onto a file
# with mixed terminators reproduces the bug. Measured both files both ways.
#
# So the 36 bare LFs were substituted (not deleted) with a single space,
# in binary, with no CSV round-trip: eb01aca5… -> dcf9b9e5…, byte count
# unchanged at 857,600 because the substitution is 1:1. Deletion was the
# wrong move — 30 of the 36 sit between two non-space characters and would
# have fused word pairs. The 12 intra-field CRLFs were left alone; they
# round-trip correctly under this rule, being CRLF on both sides.
#
# With bare LFs at zero the file is now structurally identical to the
# corpus above, and `eol=crlf` makes every platform check out the bytes
# dcf9b9e5… names. No manifest pins this file yet — the rule exists so
# that the first one to do so records a digest that survives a clone.
samples/20260816_1841_rsaAggregate.csv text eol=crlf
# Wave 17b's experiment arms. `experiment_spec.json` records a `source_sha256`
# for every arm, and a recorded digest is a promise about bytes (F-224) — so the
# bytes have to survive checkout unchanged or the promise is platform-dependent.
#
# `eol=crlf` rather than `binary`, and the direction matters here for the same
# reason it does for the corpus rule above: the digests were computed from the
# working-tree form, which is CRLF, so pinning the index bytes (LF) would invert
# the failure rather than remove it. Every arm file is pure-CRLF with zero bare
# LFs, so the round trip is exact.
#
# MEASURED, and the reason this rule exists at all: wave16_arms has no such rule
# and 7 of its 8 recorded `source_sha256` values are the LF form, so they do not
# match a Windows checkout today. Nothing reads them at test time, so it is
# silent — but it is the same defect, one directory over, and this rule is what
# stops wave 17 shipping it too.
docs/data/wave17_arms/*.txt text eol=crlf
docs/data/wave17_arms/*.csv text eol=crlf
docs/data/wave17_arms/*.json text eol=crlf
# Wave 16a's experiment arms (F-230, repaired at wave 17b). `experiment_spec.json`
# records a `source_sha256` for every arm, and seven of the eight were computed
# from the LF form — so on Windows, where `* text=auto` checks these out as CRLF,
# the recorded digests did not match the files they name. Nothing read them at
# test time, so it was silent for two waves.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than `eol=crlf`, and the direction is the whole point: the
# digests are correct AS MEASUREMENTS of what wave 16a consumed. Only the
# checkout form was wrong. Pinning LF makes every platform serve the bytes the
# spec already names, so this repairs seven records by editing none of them —
# which is the rule for an output artefact (F-225's INPUTS GET FIXED, OUTPUTS
# GET PRESERVED: a recorded digest is an output record).
docs/data/wave16_arms/** eol=lf

# The eighth arm is the exception the rule above cannot reach, and it is the
# same defect pointing the other way. `arm0_baseline`'s source is not under
# wave16_arms at all — it is the shipped VR sample, and its digest was computed
# from the CRLF form. Under `* text=auto` that is what Windows serves and what
# Linux does not, so the arm validated here and failed there.
#
# CORRECTION, wave 17c. The line this comment replaced claimed that "wave 17b's
# `h0_baseline` names the same file, so one rule settles both waves." **It does
# not.** Wave 17b's h0 names the RSA criteria file — 20260816_1841_ — not the VR
# one; two similarly-named files under one directory, conflated while writing
# the fix. The result was that wave 16 was repaired and wave 17 was left
# unpinned, CI went red on 12 of 16 jobs, and every Windows job passed. The rule
# below this one is the other half, and the pair is the whole of it. A fix that
# names the file it repairs is not the same as a fix that covers the class.
samples/20260122_1654_sampleIcEc.txt text eol=crlf

# The RSA criteria file, wave 17b's `h0_baseline` — the half the correction
# above was missing. Its digest 62e59ab8… is the CRLF form, and the index holds
# LF, so a Linux or macOS checkout served 11c46ceb… and the F-230 guard failed
# there on its first real outing, exactly as designed.
#
# `eol=crlf` and not `eol=lf`, for two reasons that agree. It matches the
# already-recorded digest, so it repairs the record by changing how the record
# is served rather than by rewriting a measurement (F-225: a recorded digest is
# an output record). And it is the local convention — both sample aggregates and
# the VR criteria file above are pinned `text eol=crlf`, so the whole of
# samples/ that anything pins now serves CRLF everywhere.
samples/20260816_1841_rsaSampleIcEc.txt text eol=crlf
